msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17813
msgid "'`llvm.fabs.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17817
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.fabs` on any floating-"
"point or vector of floating-point type. Not all targets support all types "
"however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17831
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.fabs.*`' intrinsics return the absolute value of the operand."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17841
msgid ""
"This function returns the same values as the libm `fabs` functions would, "
"and handles error conditions in the same way. The returned value is "
"completely identical to the input except for the sign bit; in particular, if "
"the input is a NaN, then the quiet/signaling bit and payload are perfectly "
"preserved."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17849
msgid "Floating-point min/max intrinsics comparison"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17851
msgid ""
"LLVM supports three pairs of floating-point min/max intrinsics, which differ "
"in their handling of {ref}`NaN values <floatnan>`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17854
msgid ""
"`llvm.minimum` and `llvm.maximum`: Return NaN if one of the arguments is NaN."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17856
msgid ""
"`llvm.minimumnum` and `llvm.maximumnum`: Return the other argument if one of "
"the arguments is NaN."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17858
msgid ""
"`llvm.minnum` and `llvm.maxnum`: For quiet NaNs behaves like minimumnum/"
"maximumnum. For signaling NaNs, non-deterministically returns NaN or the "
"other operand."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17862
msgid ""
"Additionally, each of these intrinsics supports two behaviors for signed "
"zeros. By default, -0.0 is considered smaller than +0.0. If the `nsz` flag "
"is specified, the order is non-deterministic: If the two inputs are zeros "
"with opposite sign, either input may be returned."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17867
msgid ""
"The mapping between the LLVM intrinsics, C functions and IEEE 754 functions "
"is as follows (up to divergences permitted by the usual {ref}`NaN rules "
"<floatnan>`):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17873
msgid "LLVM intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17874
msgid "llvm.minnum with nsz flag"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17875
msgid "llvm.minimum"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17876
msgid "llvm.minimumnum"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17878
msgid "C function"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17881
msgid "fminimum_num"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17883
msgid "IEEE 754 function"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17884
msgid "minNum (2008)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17885
msgid "minimum (2019)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17886
msgid "minimumNumber (2019)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17891
msgid "'`llvm.minnum.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17895
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.minnum` on any floating-"
"point or vector of floating-point type. Not all targets support all types "
"however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17909
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.minnum.*`' intrinsics return the minimum of the two arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17920
msgid ""
"If both operands are qNaNs, returns a {ref}`NaN <floatnan>`. If one operand "
"is qNaN and the other operand is a number, returns the number. If both "
"operands are numbers, returns the lesser of the two arguments. -0.0 is "
"considered to be less than +0.0 for this intrinsic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17925 ../../../LangRef.md:17985
msgid ""
"If an operand is a signaling NaN, then the intrinsic will non-"
"deterministically either:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17928 ../../../LangRef.md:17988
msgid "Return a {ref}`NaN <floatnan>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17929 ../../../LangRef.md:17989
msgid "Or treat the signaling NaN as a quiet NaN."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17931
msgid ""
"If the `nsz` flag is specified, `llvm.minnum` with one +0.0 and one -0.0 "
"operand may non-deterministically return either operand. Contrary to normal "
"`nsz` semantics, if both operands have the same sign, the result must also "
"have the same sign."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17936
msgid ""
"When used with the `nsz` flag, this intrinsic follows the semantics of "
"`fmin` in C and `minNum` in IEEE 754-2008, except for signaling NaN inputs, "
"which follow {ref}`LLVM's usual signaling NaN behavior <floatnan>` instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17940
msgid ""
"The `llvm.minnum` intrinsic can be refined into `llvm.minimumnum`, as the "
"latter exhibits a subset of behaviors of the former."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17944 ../../../LangRef.md:18004
msgid ""
"If the intrinsic is used without nsz, not all backends currently respect the "
"specified signed zero ordering. Do not rely on it until this warning has "
"been removed. See [issue #174730](https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/"
"issues/174730)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17951
msgid "'`llvm.maxnum.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17955
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.maxnum` on any floating-"
"point or vector of floating-point type. Not all targets support all types "
"however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17969
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.maxnum.*`' intrinsics return the maximum of the two arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17980
msgid ""
"If both operands are qNaNs, returns a {ref}`NaN <floatnan>`. If one operand "
"is qNaN and the other operand is a number, returns the number. If both "
"operands are numbers, returns the greater of the two arguments. -0.0 is "
"considered to be less than +0.0 for this intrinsic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17991
msgid ""
"If the `nsz` flag is specified, `llvm.maxnum` with one +0.0 and one -0.0 "
"operand may non-deterministically return either operand. Contrary to normal "
"`nsz` semantics, if both operands have the same sign, the result must also "
"have the same sign."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:17996
msgid ""
"When used with the `nsz` flag, this intrinsic follows the semantics of "
"`fmax` in C and `maxNum` in IEEE 754-2008, except for signaling NaN inputs, "
"which follow {ref}`LLVM's usual signaling NaN behavior <floatnan>` instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18000
msgid ""
"The `llvm.maxnum` intrinsic can be refined into `llvm.maximumnum`, as the "
"latter exhibits a subset of behaviors of the former."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18011
msgid "'`llvm.minimum.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18015
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.minimum` on any floating-"
"point or vector of floating-point type. Not all targets support all types "
"however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18029
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.minimum.*`' intrinsics return the minimum of the two arguments, "
"propagating NaNs and treating -0.0 as less than +0.0."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18039
msgid ""
"If either operand is a NaN, returns a {ref}`NaN <floatnan>`. Otherwise "
"returns the lesser of the two arguments. -0.0 is considered to be less than "
"+0.0 for this intrinsic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18043
msgid ""
"This intrinsic follows the semantics of `fminimum` in C23 and `minimum` in "
"IEEE 754-2019, except for signaling NaN inputs, which follow {ref}`LLVM's "
"usual signaling NaN behavior <floatnan>` instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18047
msgid ""
"If the `nsz` flag is specified, `llvm.minimum` with one +0.0 and one -0.0 "
"operand may non-deterministically return either operand. Contrary to normal "
"`nsz` semantics, if both operands have the same sign, the result must also "
"have the same sign."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18054
msgid "'`llvm.maximum.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18058
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.maximum` on any floating-"
"point or vector of floating-point type. Not all targets support all types "
"however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18072
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.maximum.*`' intrinsics return the maximum of the two arguments, "
"propagating NaNs and treating -0.0 as less than +0.0."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18082
msgid ""
"If either operand is a NaN, returns a {ref}`NaN <floatnan>`. Otherwise "
"returns the greater of the two arguments. -0.0 is considered to be less than "
"+0.0 for this intrinsic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18086
msgid ""
"This intrinsic follows the semantics of `fmaximum` in C23 and `maximum` in "
"IEEE 754-2019, except for signaling NaN inputs, which follow {ref}`LLVM's "
"usual signaling NaN behavior <floatnan>` instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18090
msgid ""
"If the `nsz` flag is specified, `llvm.maximum` with one +0.0 and one -0.0 "
"operand may non-deterministically return either operand. Contrary to normal "
"`nsz` semantics, if both operands have the same sign, the result must also "
"have the same sign."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18097
msgid "'`llvm.minimumnum.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18101
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.minimumnum` on any "
"floating-point or vector of floating-point type. Not all targets support all "
"types however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18115
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.minimumnum.*`' intrinsics return the minimum of the two "
"arguments, not propagating NaNs and treating -0.0 as less than +0.0."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18126
msgid ""
"If both operands are NaNs (including sNaN), returns a {ref}`NaN <floatnan>`. "
"If one operand is NaN (including sNaN) and the other operand is a number, "
"returns the number. Otherwise returns the lesser of the two arguments. -0.0 "
"is considered to be less than +0.0 for this intrinsic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18131
msgid ""
"If the `nsz` flag is specified, `llvm.minimumnum` with one +0.0 and one -0.0 "
"operand may non-deterministically return either operand. Contrary to normal "
"`nsz` semantics, if both operands have the same sign, the result must also "
"have the same sign."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18136
msgid ""
"This intrinsic follows the semantics of `fminimum_num` in C23 and "
"`minimumNumber` in IEEE 754-2019, except for signaling NaN inputs, which "
"follow {ref}`LLVM's usual signaling NaN behavior <floatnan>` instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18140
msgid ""
"This intrinsic behaves the same as `llvm.minnum` other than its treatment of "
"sNaN inputs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18145
msgid "'`llvm.maximumnum.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18149
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.maximumnum` on any "
"floating-point or vector of floating-point type. Not all targets support all "
"types however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18163
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.maximumnum.*`' intrinsics return the maximum of the two "
"arguments, not propagating NaNs and treating -0.0 as less than +0.0."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18174
msgid ""
"If both operands are NaNs (including sNaN), returns a {ref}`NaN <floatnan>`. "
"If one operand is NaN (including sNaN) and the other operand is a number, "
"returns the number. Otherwise returns the greater of the two arguments. -0.0 "
"is considered to be less than +0.0 for this intrinsic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18180
msgid ""
"If the `nsz` flag is specified, `llvm.maximumnum` with one +0.0 and one -0.0 "
"operand may non-deterministically return either operand. Contrary to normal "
"`nsz` semantics, if both operands have the same sign, the result must also "
"have the same sign."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18185
msgid ""
"This intrinsic follows the semantics of `fmaximum_num` in C23 and "
"`maximumNumber` in IEEE 754-2019, except for signaling NaN inputs, which "
"follow {ref}`LLVM's usual signaling NaN behavior <floatnan>` instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18189
msgid ""
"This intrinsic behaves the same as `llvm.maxnum` other than its treatment of "
"sNaN inputs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18194
msgid "'`llvm.copysign.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18198
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.copysign` on any floating-"
"point or vector of floating-point type. Not all targets support all types "
"however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18212
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.copysign.*`' intrinsics return a value with the magnitude of the "
"first operand and the sign of the second operand."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18222
msgid ""
"This function returns the same values as the libm `copysign` functions "
"would, and handles error conditions in the same way. The returned value is "
"completely identical to the first operand except for the sign bit; in "
"particular, if the input is a NaN, then the quiet/signaling bit and payload "
"are perfectly preserved."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18230
msgid "'`llvm.floor.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18234
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.floor` on any floating-"
"point or vector of floating-point type. Not all targets support all types "
"however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18248
msgid "The '`llvm.floor.*`' intrinsics return the floor of the operand."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18257
msgid ""
"This function returns the same values as the libm `floor` functions would, "
"and handles error conditions in the same way."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18262
msgid "'`llvm.ceil.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18266
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.ceil` on any floating-"
"point or vector of floating-point type. Not all targets support all types "
"however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18280
msgid "The '`llvm.ceil.*`' intrinsics return the ceiling of the operand."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18289
msgid ""
"This function returns the same values as the libm `ceil` functions would, "
"and handles error conditions in the same way."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18295
msgid "'`llvm.trunc.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18299
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.trunc` on any floating-"
"point or vector of floating-point type. Not all targets support all types "
"however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18313
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.trunc.*`' intrinsics returns the operand rounded to the nearest "
"integer not larger in magnitude than the operand."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18323
msgid ""
"This function returns the same values as the libm `trunc` functions would, "
"and handles error conditions in the same way."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18328
msgid "'`llvm.rint.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18332
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.rint` on any floating-"
"point or vector of floating-point type. Not all targets support all types "
"however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18346
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.rint.*`' intrinsics returns the operand rounded to the nearest "
"integer. It may raise an inexact floating-point exception if the operand "
"isn't an integer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18357
msgid ""
"This function returns the same values as the libm `rint` functions would, "
"and handles error conditions in the same way. Since LLVM assumes the {ref}"
"`default floating-point environment <floatenv>`, the rounding mode is "
"assumed to be set to \"nearest\", so halfway cases are rounded to the even "
"integer. Use {ref}`Constrained Floating-Point Intrinsics <constrainedfp>` to "
"avoid that assumption."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18366
msgid "'`llvm.nearbyint.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18370
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.nearbyint` on any "
"floating-point or vector of floating-point type. Not all targets support all "
"types however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18384
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.nearbyint.*`' intrinsics returns the operand rounded to the "
"nearest integer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18394
msgid ""
"This function returns the same values as the libm `nearbyint` functions "
"would, and handles error conditions in the same way. Since LLVM assumes the "
"{ref}`default floating-point environment <floatenv>`, the rounding mode is "
"assumed to be set to \"nearest\", so halfway cases are rounded to the even "
"integer. Use {ref}`Constrained Floating-Point Intrinsics <constrainedfp>` to "
"avoid that assumption."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18403
msgid "'`llvm.round.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18407
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.round` on any floating-"
"point or vector of floating-point type. Not all targets support all types "
"however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18421
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.round.*`' intrinsics returns the operand rounded to the nearest "
"integer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18431
msgid ""
"This function returns the same values as the libm `round` functions would, "
"and handles error conditions in the same way."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18436
msgid "'`llvm.roundeven.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18440
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.roundeven` on any "
"floating-point or vector of floating-point type. Not all targets support all "
"types however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18454
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.roundeven.*`' intrinsics returns the operand rounded to the "
"nearest integer in floating-point format rounding halfway cases to even "
"(that is, to the nearest value that is an even integer)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18464
msgid ""
"This function implements IEEE 754 operation `roundToIntegralTiesToEven`. It "
"also behaves in the same way as C standard function `roundeven`, including "
"that it disregards rounding mode and does not raise floating point "
"exceptions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18469
msgid "'`llvm.lround.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18473
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.lround` on any floating-"
"point type or vector of floating-point type. Not all targets support all "
"types however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18493
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.lround.*`' intrinsics return the operand rounded to the nearest "
"integer with ties away from zero."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18499 ../../../LangRef.md:18531
#: ../../../LangRef.md:18573 ../../../LangRef.md:18608
msgid ""
"The argument is a floating-point number and the return value is an integer "
"type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18504
msgid ""
"This function returns the same values as the libm `lround` functions would, "
"but without setting errno. If the rounded value is too large to be stored in "
"the result type, the return value is a non-deterministic value (equivalent "
"to `freeze poison`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18509
msgid "'`llvm.llround.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18513
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.llround` on any floating-"
"point type. Not all targets support all types however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18526
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.llround.*`' intrinsics return the operand rounded to the nearest "
"integer with ties away from zero."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18536
msgid ""
"This function returns the same values as the libm `llround` functions would, "
"but without setting errno. If the rounded value is too large to be stored in "
"the result type, the return value is a non-deterministic value (equivalent "
"to `freeze poison`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18543
msgid "'`llvm.lrint.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18547
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.lrint` on any floating-"
"point type or vector of floating-point type. Not all targets support all "
"types however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18567
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.lrint.*`' intrinsics return the operand rounded to the nearest "
"integer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18578
msgid ""
"This function returns the same values as the libm `lrint` functions would, "
"but without setting errno. If the rounded value is too large to be stored in "
"the result type, the return value is a non-deterministic value (equivalent "
"to `freeze poison`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18585
msgid "'`llvm.llrint.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18589
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.llrint` on any floating-"
"point type or vector of floating-point type. Not all targets support all "
"types however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18603
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.llrint.*`' intrinsics return the operand rounded to the nearest "
"integer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18613
msgid ""
"This function returns the same values as the libm `llrint` functions would, "
"but without setting errno. If the rounded value is too large to be stored in "
"the result type, the return value is a non-deterministic value (equivalent "
"to `freeze poison`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18618
msgid "Bit Manipulation Intrinsics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18620
msgid ""
"LLVM provides intrinsics for a few important bit manipulation operations. "
"These allow efficient code generation for some algorithms."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18625
msgid "'`llvm.bitreverse.*`' Intrinsics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18629
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic function. You can use bitreverse on any "
"integer type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18641
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.bitreverse`' family of intrinsics is used to reverse the "
"bitpattern of an integer value or vector of integer values; for example "
"`0b10110110` becomes `0b01101101`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18647
msgid ""
"The `llvm.bitreverse.iN` intrinsic returns an iN value that has bit `M` in "
"the input moved to bit `N-M-1` in the output. The vector intrinsics, such as "
"`llvm.bitreverse.v4i32`, operate on a per-element basis and the element "
"order is not affected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18654
msgid "'`llvm.bswap.*`' Intrinsics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18658
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic function. You can use bswap on any integer "
"type that is an even number of bytes (i.e., BitWidth % 16 == 0)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18670
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.bswap`' family of intrinsics is used to byte swap an integer "
"value or vector of integer values with an even number of bytes (positive "
"multiple of 16 bits)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18676
msgid ""
"The `llvm.bswap.i16` intrinsic returns an i16 value that has the high and "
"low byte of the input i16 swapped. Similarly, the `llvm.bswap.i32` intrinsic "
"returns an i32 value that has the four bytes of the input i32 swapped, so "
"that if the input bytes are numbered 0, 1, 2, 3 then the returned i32 will "
"have its bytes in 3, 2, 1, 0 order. The `llvm.bswap.i48`, `llvm.bswap.i64` "
"and other intrinsics extend this concept to additional even-byte lengths (6 "
"bytes, 8 bytes and more, respectively). The vector intrinsics, such as "
"`llvm.bswap.v4i32`, operate on a per-element basis and the element order is "
"not affected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18688
msgid "'`llvm.ctpop.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18692
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.ctpop` on any integer bit "
"width, or on any vector with integer elements. Not all targets support all "
"bit widths or vector types, however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18707
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.ctpop`' family of intrinsics counts the number of bits set in a "
"value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18712
msgid ""
"The only argument is the value to be counted. The argument may be of any "
"integer type, or a vector with integer elements. The return type must match "
"the argument type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18718
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.ctpop`' intrinsic counts the 1's in a variable, or within each "
"element of a vector."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18723
msgid "'`llvm.ctlz.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18727
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.ctlz` on any integer bit "
"width, or any vector whose elements are integers. Not all targets support "
"all bit widths or vector types, however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18738
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.ctlz`' family of intrinsic functions counts the number of leading "
"zeros in a variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18743 ../../../LangRef.md:18784
msgid ""
"The first argument is the value to be counted. This argument may be of any "
"integer type, or a vector with integer element type. The return type must "
"match the first argument type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18747 ../../../LangRef.md:18788
msgid ""
"The second argument is a constant flag that indicates whether the intrinsic "
"returns a valid result if the first argument is zero. If the first argument "
"is zero and the second argument is true, the result is poison. Historically "
"some architectures did not provide a defined result for zero values as "
"efficiently, and many algorithms are now predicated on avoiding zero-value "
"inputs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18756
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.ctlz`' intrinsic counts the leading (most significant) zeros in a "
"variable, or within each element of the vector. If `src == 0` then the "
"result is the size in bits of the type of `src` if `is_zero_poison == 0` and "
"`poison` otherwise. For example, `llvm.ctlz(i32 2) = 30`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18764
msgid "'`llvm.cttz.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18768
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.cttz` on any integer bit "
"width, or any vector of integer elements. Not all targets support all bit "
"widths or vector types, however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18779
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.cttz`' family of intrinsic functions counts the number of "
"trailing zeros."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18797
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.cttz`' intrinsic counts the trailing (least significant) zeros in "
"a variable, or within each element of a vector. If `src == 0` then the "
"result is the size in bits of the type of `src` if `is_zero_poison == 0` and "
"`poison` otherwise. For example, `llvm.cttz(2) = 1`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18805
msgid "'`llvm.fshl.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18809
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.fshl` on any integer bit "
"width or any vector of integer elements. Not all targets support all bit "
"widths or vector types, however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18821
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.fshl`' family of intrinsic functions performs a funnel shift "
"left: the first two values are concatenated as { %a : %b } (%a is the most "
"significant bits of the wide value), the combined value is shifted left, and "
"the most significant bits are extracted to produce a result that is the same "
"size as the original arguments. If the first 2 arguments are identical, this "
"is equivalent to a rotate left operation. For vector types, the operation "
"occurs for each element of the vector. The shift argument is treated as an "
"unsigned amount modulo the element size of the arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18832 ../../../LangRef.md:18875
msgid ""
"The first two arguments are the values to be concatenated. The third "
"argument is the shift amount. The arguments may be any integer type or a "
"vector with integer element type. All arguments and the return value must "
"have the same type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18848
msgid "'`llvm.fshr.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18852
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.fshr` on any integer bit "
"width or any vector of integer elements. Not all targets support all bit "
"widths or vector types, however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18864
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.fshr`' family of intrinsic functions performs a funnel shift "
"right: the first two values are concatenated as { %a : %b } (%a is the most "
"significant bits of the wide value), the combined value is shifted right, "
"and the least significant bits are extracted to produce a result that is the "
"same size as the original arguments. If the first 2 arguments are identical, "
"this is equivalent to a rotate right operation. For vector types, the "
"operation occurs for each element of the vector. The shift argument is "
"treated as an unsigned amount modulo the element size of the arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18891
msgid "'`llvm.clmul.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18895
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.clmul` on any integer or "
"vectors of integer elements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18907
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.clmul`' family of intrinsic functions performs carry-less "
"multiplication, or XOR multiplication, on the two arguments, and returns the "
"low-bits."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18913 ../../../LangRef.md:18955
#: ../../../LangRef.md:18997 ../../../LangRef.md:20219
#: ../../../LangRef.md:20258
msgid ""
"The arguments may be any integer type or vector of integer type. Both "
"arguments and result must have the same type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18918
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.clmul`' intrinsic computes carry-less multiply of its arguments, "
"which is the result of applying the standard multiplication algorithm, where "
"all of the additions are replaced with XORs, and returns the low-bits. The "
"vector variants operate lane-wise."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18934
msgid "'`llvm.pext.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18938
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.pext` on any integer or "
"integer vector type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18949
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.pext`' family of intrinsic functions extracts the bits from `val` "
"where the `mask` has bits set and packs them contiguously in the low bits of "
"the result, same as the x86 `PEXT` instruction."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18960
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.pext`' intrinsic extracts bits from the first argument `val` at "
"the positions indicated by set bits in `mask`, and packs them contiguously "
"into the low bits of the result. The remaining high bits of the result are "
"zero."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18965
msgid ""
"Equivalently, if the set bit positions in `mask` (from LSB to MSB) are `p0, "
"p1, ..., pk`, then the result bit `i` equals bit `pi` of `val`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18976
msgid "'`llvm.pdep.*`' Intrinsic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18980
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.pdep` on any integer or "
"integer vector type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:18991
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.pdep`' family of intrinsic functions deposits the low bits of "
"`val` into the result at the positions where `mask` has bits set, same as "
"the x86 `PDEP` instruction."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:19002
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.pdep`' intrinsic takes the low bits of the first argument `val` "
"and scatters them to the bit positions in the result indicated by set bits "
"in `mask`. Bits in the result at positions where `mask` is zero are zero."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:19007
msgid ""
"Equivalently, if the set bit positions in `mask` (from LSB to MSB) are `p0, "
"p1, ..., pk`, then result bit `pi` equals bit `i` of `val`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:19010
msgid ""
"The operations satisfy the round-trip identity: `pdep(pext(val, mask), mask) "
"== val & mask`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:19020
msgid "Arithmetic with Overflow Intrinsics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:19022
msgid "LLVM provides intrinsics for fast arithmetic overflow checking."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:19024
msgid ""
"Each of these intrinsics returns a two-element struct. The first element of "
"this struct contains the result of the corresponding arithmetic operation "
"modulo 2{sup}`n`, where n is the bit width of the result. Therefore, for "
"example, the first element of the struct returned by "
"`llvm.sadd.with.overflow.i32` is always the same as the result of a 32-bit "
"`add` instruction with the same operands, where the `add` is *not* modified "
"by an `nsw` or `nuw` flag."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:19032
msgid ""
"The second element of the result is an `i1` that is 1 if the arithmetic "
"operation overflowed and 0 otherwise. An operation overflows if, for any "
"values of its operands `A` and `B` and for any `N` larger than the operands' "
"width, `ext(A op B) to iN` is not equal to `(ext(A) to iN) op (ext(B) to "
"iN)` where `ext` is `sext` for signed overflow and `zext` for unsigned "
"overflow, and `op` is the underlying arithmetic operation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:19040
msgid ""
"The behavior of these intrinsics is well-defined for all argument values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:19043
msgid "'`llvm.sadd.with.overflow.*`' Intrinsics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:19047
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.sadd.with.overflow` on "
"any integer bit width or vectors of integers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:19059
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.sadd.with.overflow`' family of intrinsic functions perform a "
"signed addition of the two arguments, and indicate whether an overflow "
"occurred during the signed summation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:19065
msgid ""
"The arguments (%a and %b) and the first element of the result structure may "
"be of integer types of any bit width, but they must have the same bit width. "
"The second element of the result structure must be of type `i1`. `%a` and "
"`%b` are the two values that will undergo signed addition."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:19073
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.sadd.with.overflow`' family of intrinsic functions perform a "
"signed addition of the two variables. They return a structure --- the first "
"element of which is the signed summation, and the second element of which is "
"a bit specifying if the signed summation resulted in an overflow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:19088
msgid "'`llvm.uadd.with.overflow.*`' Intrinsics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:19092
msgid ""
"This is an overloaded intrinsic. You can use `llvm.uadd.with.overflow` on "
"any integer bit width or vectors of integers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:19104
msgid ""
"The '`llvm.uadd.with.overflow`' family of intrinsic functions perform an "
"unsigned addition of the two arguments, and indicate whether a carry "
"occurred during the unsigned summation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LangRef.md:19110
